<h2 id="recently-shipped">Recently shipped</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Scanned books come out clean.</strong> OCR junk that no keyboard can produce — guillemets, stray carets and asterisks welded into words, clipped ellipses, trademark signs — is cleaned out of imported text, guarded so that real notation (<code>x^2</code>, <code>**bold**</code>, <code>a &lt; b</code>, scene breaks) survives. The <a href="/custom/">import preview</a> shows exactly what changed, rule by rule, with an off-switch; the choice is stored per text. Runs on display too, so books imported before the fix repair themselves on next open.</li>
  <li><strong>Running heads and page numbers stripped from scanned PDFs.</strong> A scanned book repeats its title and folio at the edge of every page, and the text layer used to hand them back mid-sentence. Page numbers are digits-only — "Il", "did" and "mix" are words, not roman numerals — and the scanner's dozens of misspellings of a head are clustered so they fall together. On the reference scan, headed pages went from 445 of 530 to 27 with chapter numbers, diary dates and refrains untouched.</li>
  <li><strong>Japanese and Chinese texts split into segments.</strong> CJK scripts put no space after a full stop, so a whole book used to arrive as one segment. Sentence boundaries are now recognized per script, and no space is invented mid-sentence when segments join.</li>
  <li><strong>Custom texts get a reader header.</strong> Title, author when known, and "Segment N of M" above the typing surface — the same treatment library books get.</li>
  <li><strong>The bundled library is typeable, and licence-free.</strong> Project Gutenberg apparatus purged from 59 books (it had even shipped as nine "chapters" of <em>At the Mountains of Madness</em>), coordinate primes and vulgar fractions mapped to keyboard characters, and a typeability census now gates the corpus — the next untypeable character fails the build, not the reader. Nine characters stay by design because they <em>are</em> the text, Poe's cryptogram and the full stop that ends <em>Ithaca</em> among them.</li>
  <li><strong>Imported text is typeable text.</strong> A book's text layer is full of characters that look like a space and are not one — non-breaking spaces, thin spaces, figure spaces — plus zero-width joiners and soft hyphens that no keyboard can produce. All of them used to reach the typing surface, where you would press the spacebar and be marked wrong. A word broken across a line ("short-<br>ened") also gained a space in the middle of it. Both are fixed at the point of import, and again on the way out, so books imported before the fix are repaired without re-importing.</li>
  <li><strong>EPUB and PDF import.</strong> Drop a <code>.epub</code> or <code>.pdf</code> on the <a href="/custom/">Custom text</a> page and type from your own books. Whole books are fine — nothing is trimmed, and you pick the segment you want. PDF text is rejoined by measuring the geometry rather than gluing fragments together, so words no longer arrive as "beca use".</li>
  <li><strong>Adaptive drills.</strong> A drill now gives you extra repetitions of the characters in that drill you are worst at, while still showing every word in the set at least once — the word list is the drill's curriculum, so nothing is dropped. Drills only lengthen once there is a real signal to act on; until then they run exactly as before.</li>
  <li><strong>Adaptive lesson curriculum.</strong> Every key-set lesson now weights its picker toward the keys and bigrams you are actually slow on, not just Lesson 20.</li>
  <li><strong>Numpad layout + drills.</strong> A full 10-key layout with correct finger mapping, six numpad drills, and numpad support in both the live keyboard and the stats heatmap.</li>
  <li><strong>Theme builder.</strong> Pick your own background / text / accent tokens, save them locally, export and import as JSON, with a WCAG contrast warning. A saved theme now follows you across the whole site — it used to apply on the settings page and vanish on the next navigation.</li>
  <li><strong>Eleven more typing fonts.</strong> Fira Code, IBM Plex Mono, Iosevka, Cascadia Code, Roboto Mono, Source Code Pro, Ubuntu Mono, Space Mono, Inconsolata and Cousine join JetBrains Mono. Iosevka is self-hosted; the rest come from Bunny.</li>
  <li><strong>Lesson trends as small multiples.</strong> One panel per lesson, showing WPM <em>and</em> accuracy across attempts, on a shared scale so panels compare directly. The old chart plotted WPM only and cut its legend off at eight lessons.</li>
  <li><strong>Custom lessons are tracked like lessons.</strong> A text you pin as a lesson now picks up where you left off, keeps its own WPM history, earns a mastery mark and best run on the lessons page, and gets its own panel — titled with the book, not an internal id — in lesson trends. It is namespaced separately from the curriculum, so an imported novel can never count toward "finish every lesson".</li>
  <li><strong>Power-ups in games.</strong> Bonus words fall alongside the ordinary ones in Catch the Word. Type one and it grants a time-freeze or clears the screen; miss it and it grants nothing.</li>
  <li><strong>Punctuation Storm.</strong> Punctuation-heavy fragments — <code>("wait,")</code>, <code>[see p. 12];</code>, <code>x = {a: 1};</code> — rain down four lanes and arrive faster the longer you last. 10 points per punctuation character, +10% per fragment in the current combo; a fragment reaching the gutter costs a shield and resets the combo. The "Skip punctuation" setting is deliberately ignored here, and the page says so.</li>
  <li><strong>Word Snake and Boss Battle.</strong> Two more games, with per-mode high scores, a health bar and a per-word countdown.</li>
  <li><strong>Live ticker.</strong> A keystroke-by-keystroke pulse strip under the typing surface, with a net-WPM readout. Opt in from settings.</li>
  <li><strong>Tape (ticker) mode.</strong> Single horizontal line of words that scrolls left as you type. JS rAF-driven slide for smooth motion; 15-second default sprint.</li>
  <li><strong>Games depth.</strong> Word Shooter (horizontal drift) and Endless (no 3-miss cap) modes. Per-mode high scores in <code>profile.gameStats.byMode</code>. Visible combo multiplier (1.5x → 5x). Score-pulse animation. "New best!" overlay with personal-best chip on each round end.</li>
  <li><strong>World-class mobile toolbar.</strong> Stats row + "current mode" summary chip. Tapping opens a 62 vh bottom sheet with every mode / variant / source. No more horizontal chip strip.</li>
  <li><strong>Tap-to-type pause guard.</strong> <code>engine.pauseTimer()</code> itself refuses to pause within 450 ms of the last keystroke — soft-keyboard taps can't bleed onto the Pause button anymore.</li>
  <li><strong>Umami analytics + public dashboard.</strong> ~50 named events covering session lifecycle, modes, library, lessons, challenges, settings, perf, errors. The full dashboard is embedded on <a href="/analytics/">/analytics/</a>.</li>
  <li><strong>Red gorilla favicon.</strong> Brand mark re-colored to brand red across every PNG size.</li>
  <li><strong>Service-worker auto-update.</strong> <code>updateViaCache:'none'</code> + <code>controllerchange</code> auto-reload — fresh builds reach users without manual cache clears.</li>
  <li><strong>Achievements dedup.</strong> Celebration panel never shows the same achievement twice; <code>evaluate()</code> uses a Set.</li>
  <li><strong>D3 across the stats page.</strong> WPM trend, per-finger viz, per-key sortable bars, calendar heatmap with day-detail drill-down, missed-words bar chart, recent-sessions list, character report. All lazy-loaded; legacy fallback if D3 fails.</li>
  <li><strong>Catch the Word game.</strong> Falling-word mini-game driven by your missed-words list. Per-char prefix coloring, streak multiplier, pixel-disintegration animation on catch.</li>
  <li><strong>Achievements expansion.</strong> 56 → ~120 unlocks.</li>
  <li><strong>Content expansion.</strong> Quotes 452 → 874. Idioms 449 → 717. Poems 148 → 177. Books in library reached 290.</li>
  <li><strong>Universal auto-advance.</strong> 10-second countdown after any clean run.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="in-flight">In flight</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Bigram chord diagram.</strong> Visualize which bigrams chain into which trigrams. Started; the open question is whether the per-trigram data this needs is recorded anywhere yet.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="planned">Planned</h2>
<ul>
  <li>OCR for scanned PDFs. Import reads a PDF's text layer, so a scanned, image-only PDF has nothing to extract and is refused with a message rather than silently importing an empty text. Running OCR in the browser is a much larger piece of work than the parser it would sit behind.</li>
  <li>Corpus follow-ups: two books whose source texts look mismatched (<em>Penguin Island</em>, <em>John Barleycorn</em>), stray <code>&lt;i&gt;</code> tags reaching the typing surface in two others, and a duplicated <em>Turn of the Screw</em>.</li>
  <li>More games — Word Boss variants, and Punctuation Storm variants (a code-only pool, and a "quotes and brackets" pool).</li>
  <li>Theme sharing — a way to publish a saved theme so other people can import it, without adding accounts.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="deferred">Deferred</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Cloud sync of profiles. Local-first is the model; sync introduces accounts, server cost, and privacy tradeoffs that don't fit the project's posture today.</li>
  <li>Multiplayer / racing. Adds substantial network + matchmaking complexity for a feature plenty of other sites already do well.</li>
  <li>Built-in voice or video coaching. The typing surface is the product; layered media expands surface area without expanding the core practice loop.</li>
</ul>

